WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.680
DALE LEWIS: We are
British Telecoms.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:11.690
We are one of the largest UK
network and telecommunications

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:12.390
providers.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:16.309
We also operate globally
across 180 different countries.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:19.620
And we work specifically
in BT digital.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.530
And really, we are
focused on taking BT

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:23.840
from being a telco
into a techco.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.600
So it really means
transforming core structures

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.575
and technologies, including
the drive to cloud adoption.

00:00:29.575 --> 00:00:30.950
DANIEL RANDELL:
Our key goals are

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:32.810
to align with moving
away from our legacy

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:35.300
tightly-coupled architecture
with our on-premise data

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.870
warehouses and towards a
loosely-coupled managed data

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:42.230
mesh architecture that will
enable new and exciting value

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:43.100
from our data.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.350
RYAN FLYNN: Each week, we
have around 30 requests

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:46.220
that have to be provisioned.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.050
And it would take an engineer
around three hours to do.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.217
We knew that with the
[INAUDIBLE] adoption GCP about

00:00:50.217 --> 00:00:53.300
to begin in the business, we had
to have a solution that can not

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.670
only react to the potential
100 project requests,

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:57.410
but also react to
this demand to build

00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:58.873
a project as soon as possible.

00:00:58.873 --> 00:01:00.290
For a team of only
four engineers,

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:01.707
this is a huge
undertaking that we

00:01:01.707 --> 00:01:03.260
had to do within three months.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.150
DALE LEWIS: Our DevOps
philosophy really

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:06.660
is that it's a must have.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:07.748
It's a core belief.

00:01:07.748 --> 00:01:09.290
It's something we
know we have to do.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:10.707
We have to be able
to move faster.

00:01:10.707 --> 00:01:12.650
We have to be able
to change quickly.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.817
To do that safely, we
need to use DevOps.

00:01:15.817 --> 00:01:17.150
We need to really understand it.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:18.560
We need to build,
measure, learn.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:19.520
And I'm not just saying that.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.130
We need to physically look at
the metrics we're producing,

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:23.720
get that continuous
feedback loop.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.242
And DevOps is at the core of
everything we do within BT.

00:01:26.242 --> 00:01:28.700
DANIEL RANDELL: We reached out
to collaborate with a Google

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:30.770
Cloud team of
[INAUDIBLE] and engineers

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.170
to ask them if some of the
problems that we faced,

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.483
it had been had elsewhere.

00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:38.150
We were put into contact with an
engineer, who helped to give us

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:39.475
a jump start solution.

00:01:39.475 --> 00:01:40.850
And with that, we
saw the ability

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.490
to see what was possible and
how we could quickly redevelop

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.992
and expand that into the
solution it is today.

00:01:45.992 --> 00:01:48.200
RYAN FLYNN: It was interesting
to see the true limits

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:49.190
of our automation.

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:52.010
In 24 hours, we managed to
create 1,000 projects using

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:54.380
automation in order to handle
the surge requests that

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.050
came as a result of
GCP adoption in BT.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.180
DALE LEWIS: We'd
like to say thank

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:02.540
you to Google first for the
technology and the work we

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:03.650
get to do.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:05.690
I'd like to say a big
thank you to my squad.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:07.190
You've met Dan and Ryan here.

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:09.620
But we also have
another engineer

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.645
who's been supporting
us and a new joiner who

00:02:11.645 --> 00:02:12.900
recently joined the team to--

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.442
we're not doing
something small here.

00:02:14.442 --> 00:02:17.000
We're migrating the largest
customer database in the UK.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.950
And we've got so
much value to find.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.410
And BT has tens of thousands
of analysts and data

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.882
scientists ready to go.

00:02:23.882 --> 00:02:25.340
And this automation
is what's going

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:26.798
to give us the
speed and efficiency

00:02:26.798 --> 00:02:28.910
to enable them to get
a hold of our data.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:31.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

